#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125361} :activity/id #uuid "e6e3df9f-205d-5b79-a182-6a881367dca2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048769 :topic/id #uuid "bd333eb5-72e6-5eaa-be12-c6bf36306b90" :topic/math-academy-id 1910} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125768 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf x \\in V$ for some vector space $V,$ and that $\\mathcal B$ and $\\mathcal C$ are two bases of $V.$ \n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ given its coordinates relative to $\\mathcal B,$ we can use the **change-of-basis formula**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [\\,x\\,]_{B},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we recall that\n\n- $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ respectively, and\n\n- $P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ whose columns are the coordinates of vectors of the basis $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nAs an example, suppose we are given two bases $\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\}$ of a vector space $V,$ and we already know that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nLet $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ Then we can easily find $[\\,\\mathbf{v}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,v\\,]_{C} & =\\overset{{P}_{B\\to C}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{[v]_{B}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "827e9581-9fde-5941-ad91-2c364796e5b1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3217 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "910841a1-0516-546a-8aac-f188c965b763" :step/math-academy-id 17537 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125500}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125362 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124308 :question/id #uuid "922b0f79-2c87-56f9-bf8a-18711a2a09d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4838"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394435}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "ad493885-054b-5d5e-a186-80a2c3801b8b" :step/math-academy-id 17538 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125363}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125363 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124309 :question/id #uuid "a3911ca2-a357-5f37-8abc-e01e5518f24b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4840"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394442}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "45243fec-9ed2-54d8-b6ef-0d2c0a1e7087" :step/math-academy-id 17541 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125364}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125364 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124310 :question/id #uuid "a2d3629f-5b7c-5b65-998f-8754ff11e7b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4841"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394421}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"} :step/id #uuid "9fa2d20c-055f-5364-a1f7-12e38362db0d" :step/math-academy-id 17542 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125667}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125498 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124577 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124384 :question/id #uuid "f04f55d3-40ec-52a8-a762-d8ac7090b8b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7276"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048920}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394449}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"} :step/id #uuid "2c41659c-3fb0-58d9-904f-2296e677eb6c" :step/math-academy-id 26946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125500 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125822 :tutorial/content "We've seen that to find the coordinates of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to a basis $\\mathcal{C}$ given its coordinates relative to a basis $\\mathcal B,$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [\\,x\\,]_{B}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn expanded form, the formula looks as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ [x]_{C} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, remember that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ and from $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ are the inverses of each other:\n\n$$\nP_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}} = P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}\n\t\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad\n\tP_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}} = P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}^{-1}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cfffdf8e-9e9f-5304-8249-54a094cc7c11" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4435 :tutorial/title "The Change-of-Basis Formula in Expanded Form"} :step/id #uuid "4c4259ae-ebd7-5d31-ae41-72156cefc066" :step/math-academy-id 27272 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125362}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125708 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Now, suppose we would like to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ \n\n- We first need to find $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{c}_1+x_2 \\mathbf{c}_2 = \\mathbf{b}_1,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to solving the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, we need to find $y_1$ and $y_2$ such that \n\n$$\ny_1 \\mathbf{c}_1+y_2 \\mathbf{c}_2 = \\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to solving the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve both systems using Gaussian elimination. However, we can do that simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReducing the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, we get a matrix of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & {x}_{1} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 0 & 1 & {x}_{2} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the required coefficients will be stored in the columns on the right-hand side." :tutorial/id #uuid "d42ee304-2e1f-5cc2-b334-65d083ea7323" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13287 :tutorial/title "Using Gaussian Elimination to Find the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis"} :step/id #uuid "184b8c31-de1a-5e34-ad87-2f626c830182" :step/math-academy-id 462199 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125498}}] :activity/title "Changing a Basis Using the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124710}]}
